/* ==============================================================
   02 — MoveJoints: the joint-space move
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   What a joint move is, what the four numbers mean, and
             when to reach for it
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     All four joints start together and stop together
   TIME      ~25 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   WHAT IS A JOINT MOVE?

   An MCS500 is a SCARA: an arm that swings in a horizontal
   plane, with a vertical shaft at the end that moves up and down
   and turns. It has four joints, and a joint move tells each one
   where it should end up. Nothing else.

       MoveJoints(θ1, θ2, d3, θ4)

     θ1   joint 1, the shoulder: swings the whole arm about the
          base, in degrees
     θ2   joint 2, the elbow: swings the outer link, in degrees
     d3   joint 3, the shaft going up and down, in MILLIMETRES
     θ4   joint 4, the shaft turning about its own axis, in
          degrees

   Read that third one twice. Joint 3 is not a rotation: it is a
   straight-line slide, so its number is a distance. 0 is the
   shaft all the way up, -102 is all the way down. Every other
   joint number in this repo is an angle.

   (Joints 3 and 4 are both produced by the same ball-screw
   spline shaft, driven by two nuts — which is why the manual
   calls them "independent degrees of freedom" rather than links.
   For programming, they are simply two joints.)

   All zeros is the robot's "zero position": the arm fully
   stretched, pointing straight out in front of the base, with
   the shaft all the way up. The end of the shaft is then 225 mm
   out from the base axis (100 mm of inner link plus 125 mm of
   outer link) and 109 mm above the bench.

   This is the most direct thing you can ask a robot to do. There
   is no maths between your command and the motors: you name the
   positions, the joints go to them.

   THE PART CUSTOMERS ASK ABOUT

   The robot does NOT move each joint one after another, and it
   does not move them all at full speed until each arrives. It
   SYNCHRONISES them: every joint starts at the same instant, and
   every joint arrives at the same instant. The joint that needs
   the LONGEST TIME sets the pace; the others are slowed to
   match. That is what makes the motion look like one gesture
   instead of four.

   Note: the longest TIME, not the longest distance. On this
   robot that difference is large. Joint 4 can turn at 5,000
   deg/s; joint 1 tops out at 300 deg/s. A half turn of the shaft
   is nothing next to a quarter turn of the whole arm. The last
   move below shows it.

   THE TRADE-OFF

   Because you specified joint positions and not a position in
   space, the TOOL TIP takes whatever path falls out of the
   geometry. It is smooth and it is repeatable — run it a
   thousand times and you get the same curve — but in the
   horizontal plane it is a curve, not a straight line, and it is
   hard to predict by eye.

   So:
     - Use MoveJoints to REPOSITION. Getting from one side of the
       cell to the other, going to a park position, clearing an
       obstacle. It is the fastest and least constrained way to
       move, and it can never fail because of a singularity.
     - Use MoveLin when the PATH ITSELF matters. Sliding a part
       along a fixture, laying a bead, pushing a connector home.
       See 04_move_lin.mxprog.

   A joint move only fails for one reason: a target outside the
   joint's limits. The robot checks this before it starts moving,
   so a bad target does not produce a half-completed motion — it
   produces an error and a robot that has not moved.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


// Slow, so the demo is legible and safe to stand next to.
// This is a percentage of each joint's top speed, not a
// velocity. Raise it to 100 to show the real cycle time.
SetJointVel(15)


// --- Start from the zero position -----------------------------
// All four joints at zero: arm straight out in front, shaft up.
// Every example in basic/ starts and ends here, so you can run
// them back to back without re-jogging the robot.
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- One joint at a time --------------------------------------
// Only the first number changes, so only joint 1 turns: the whole
// arm swings 45 deg about the base. This is the clearest way to
// show someone what the four numbers actually mean.
MoveJoints(45, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)

// Now joint 2 bends the elbow 60 deg, while joint 1 stays where
// it is. Watch the end of the shaft: it swings through an arc.
// Nobody asked for that arc — it is simply what the geometry
// does when the elbow rotates.
MoveJoints(45, 60, 0, 0)

Delay(1)

// Joint 3: the shaft drops 50 mm, straight down. The one joint
// whose motion IS a straight line, and the number is in mm.
MoveJoints(45, 60, -50, 0)

Delay(1)

// Joint 4: the shaft turns a quarter turn about its own axis.
// With no tool fitted you will barely see it — watch the flat
// machined on the shaft, or stick a piece of tape on it. With a
// tool fitted, this is the joint that turns the part.
MoveJoints(45, 60, -50, 90)

Delay(1)


// --- All four at once -----------------------------------------
// The interesting one. Every joint has a different distance to
// travel:
//
//     joint 1   +45 to -45    90 deg    at up to   300 deg/s
//     joint 2   +60 to +90    30 deg    at up to   500 deg/s
//     joint 3   -50 to -20    30 mm     at up to   700 mm/s
//     joint 4   +90 to -90   180 deg    at up to 5,000 deg/s
//
// Joint 4 travels furthest, and it does not matter: it could do
// its half turn in a fraction of the time. Joint 1 needs the
// longest, so joint 1 sets the pace.
//
// Watch the start and the finish, not the middle: everything
// begins moving in the same instant and everything settles in
// the same instant. That is the synchronisation described above.
MoveJoints(-45, 90, -20, -90)

Delay(1)


// --- Back to zero ---------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Select any single MoveJoints line and press Ctrl+Enter to
      run just that line. Change one number, run it again. This
      is the fastest way to make the four-numbers idea land.

   2. Change SetJointVel(15) to SetJointVel(100) and run the whole
      file. Same path, same targets — dramatically shorter cycle.
      Speed is a setting, not a rewrite. Give it room: a SCARA at
      full speed is quick.

   3. Try MoveJoints(0, 150, 0, 0). The robot refuses and reports
      an error, because joint 2 stops at 145 deg. Note that it
      refuses BEFORE moving. Then press the run button (now red)
      to reset the error, and resume.

   4. Try MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 720), then MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0).
      The shaft turns two full turns and back, and the arm looks
      identical at both ends. Joint 4 is a motor angle, not a
      direction — 02_intermediate/05 is about exactly that.

   NEXT   03_move_pose.mxprog — naming a position in space
          instead of a set of joint positions.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
